    "body": "`secp256k1_silentpayments_recipient_prevouts_summary_create` and `secp256k1_silentpayments_recipient_scan_outputs` treated a transaction with no eligible inputs, or no taproot outputs, as an illegal argument. With the default illegal callback, that calls abort().\n\nThat contradicts the API documentation. Both functions are documented to return 0 if the transaction is not a Silent Payments transaction, and a transaction with no eligible inputs or no taproot outputs is exactly that case. It also puts the burden on callers. Anyone scanning a block or building an index has to filter these transactions out before calling the library. If they forget, a transaction someone else created can crash their process.\n\nThis PR makes both functions return 0 in these cases:\n\n- `prevouts_summary_create`: drops the `ARG_CHECK((n_pubkeys > 0) || (n_xonly_pubkeys > 0))`. With no public keys, the sum is the point at infinity, so the existing \"sum is infinity\" check already returns 0. No new code path is needed.\n- `scan_outputs`: drops the `ARG_CHECK(n_tx_outputs > 0)`. It returns 0 after all other arguments have been validated and before the scan key is loaded. `*n_found_outputs` is still set to 0.\n- `scan_outputs`: tx_outputs and found_outputs are now only checked for NULL if `n_tx_outputs > 0`, and their `SECP256K1_ARG_NONNULL` attributes are removed. This lets callers pass an empty array as NULL (e.g., data() of an empty std::vector). It matches how prevouts_summary_create and sender_create_outputs already treat empty key arrays (see the [previous \"Fixed\" entry in the CHANGELOG](https://github.com/bitcoin-core/secp256k1/blob/b88cbc7a65ab7629e13368c6c46468fc96d0d3da/CHANGELOG.md?plain=1#L11) added in PR #1932).",
